Alright — let’s strip this down to clear intuition first, then connect it to Angular 20 (zoneless).

⸻

🧠 Core Idea (Super Simple)

Think of Signals and Effects like this:

🧩 Signal = “State with awareness”

A signal is just a variable that:
	•	stores a value
	•	automatically notifies when it changes

👉 Like a smart variable.

⸻

⚡ Effect = “Automatic reaction”

An effect is a function that:
	•	runs automatically
	•	whenever the signal it depends on changes

👉 Like a watcher / listener.

⸻

🧃 Real-Life Analogy

🏠 Home Temperature System
	•	🌡️ Signal → Current room temperature
	•	❄️ Effect → AC turning ON/OFF

What happens?
	•	Temperature increases → AC turns ON
	•	Temperature decreases → AC turns OFF

You don’t manually check temperature — system reacts automatically.

⸻

🧑‍💻 Angular 20 (Zoneless) Context

Earlier (with Zone.js):
	•	Angular automatically checked everything (dirty checking)
	•	You didn’t control reactivity directly

Now (Zoneless + Signals):
	•	Angular reacts only when signals change
	•	No unnecessary checks
	•	More predictable + faster

⸻

🔧 Basic Example

Step 1: Create a Signal

import { signal } from '@angular/core';

count = signal(0);
👉 count() → get value
👉 count.set(5) → update value

Step 2: Create an Effect
import { effect } from '@angular/core';

effect(() => {
  console.log('Count changed:', this.count());
});
👉 This runs:
	•	once initially
	•	every time count changes


import { Component, signal, effect } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-counter',
  template: `
    <h2>Count: {{ count() }}</h2>
    <button (click)="increment()">Increment</button>
  `
})
export class CounterComponent {

  count = signal(0);

  constructor() {
    effect(() => {
      console.log('Effect triggered. Count is:', this.count());
    });
  }

  increment() {
    this.count.update(c => c + 1);
  }
}



⸻

🔄 What Happens Internally
	1.	UI reads count() → Angular tracks dependency
	2.	Button click → count updates
	3.	Angular knows:
	•	UI depends on count
	•	effect depends on count
	4.	Only those parts update

👉 No global change detection

⸻

🧠 Key Difference (Important)

Concept
Purpose
Signal
Holds state
Effect
Reacts to state change
Computed
Derives state (like formula)

🚀 1. signal() – Reactive State (Foundation)

You already saw this, but let’s anchor it in a real scenario.

🧾 Scenario: Counter / UI State

count = signal(0);

increment() {
  this.count.update(c => c + 1);
}

👉 UI updates automatically without Zone.js.

🧠 Use when:
	•	Local UI state
	•	Component-level data

⚖️ RxJS equivalent:
	•	BehaviorSubject

⸻

⚡ 2. computed() – Derived State (VERY IMPORTANT)

🧾 Scenario: Cart Total

price = signal(100);
quantity = signal(2);

total = computed(() => this.price() * this.quantity());

